1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,912
Sweetheart, on Christmas Eve of nineteen seventy-five,

2
00:00:03,912 --> 00:00:06,086
I was six dollars short at the

3
00:00:06,086 --> 00:00:09,347
grocery checkout, with two tired children and

4
00:00:09,347 --> 00:00:12,027
a line of impatient people behind me.

5
00:00:12,207 --> 00:00:14,325
I was about to put back the

6
00:00:14,325 --> 00:00:15,423
Christmas ham.

7
00:00:15,603 --> 00:00:17,938
And a gruff old farmer in overalls,

8
00:00:17,938 --> 00:00:19,672
who I'd never seen before,

9
00:00:19,672 --> 00:00:22,340
slapped a ten-dollar bill on the counter

10
00:00:22,340 --> 00:00:24,608
and walked out before I could even

11
00:00:24,608 --> 00:00:25,542
say thank you.

12
00:00:25,722 --> 00:00:27,637
I never knew his name.

13
00:00:27,817 --> 00:00:29,292
For forty years.

14
00:00:29,641 --> 00:00:31,977
Tonight I'm going to tell you what

15
00:00:31,977 --> 00:00:34,519
I did every Christmas Eve after that,

16
00:00:34,519 --> 00:00:35,618
for forty years,

17
00:00:35,618 --> 00:00:37,060
to pay that man back.

18
00:00:37,240 --> 00:00:39,284
And who I met at that same

19
00:00:39,284 --> 00:00:42,193
grocery store on Christmas Eve of two

20
00:00:42,193 --> 00:00:45,415
thousand fifteen, who finally told me his

21
00:00:45,415 --> 00:00:45,808
name.

22
00:00:45,988 --> 00:00:46,968
Stay with me,

23
00:00:46,968 --> 00:00:49,680
honey, because what she told me made

24
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:51,940
me sit down right on the floor

25
00:00:51,940 --> 00:00:53,071
of aisle three.

26
00:00:53,420 --> 00:00:55,629
It was a hard winter for us,

27
00:00:55,629 --> 00:00:56,497
sweetheart.

28
00:00:56,677 --> 00:00:59,083
The plant had cut Harold to three

29
00:00:59,083 --> 00:00:59,958
days a week,

30
00:00:59,958 --> 00:01:01,707
and we had two children,

31
00:01:01,707 --> 00:01:02,947
eleven and seven.

32
00:01:03,127 --> 00:01:05,745
I'd been saving all month for Christmas

33
00:01:05,745 --> 00:01:06,215
dinner.

34
00:01:06,395 --> 00:01:09,400
I had exactly twenty-two dollars in my

35
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:12,009
coin purse, and a list written on

36
00:01:12,009 --> 00:01:13,907
the back of an envelope,

37
00:01:13,907 --> 00:01:16,437
and I'd added it up three times.

38
00:01:16,617 --> 00:01:17,762
A small ham,

39
00:01:17,762 --> 00:01:19,860
potatoes, green beans,

40
00:01:19,860 --> 00:01:22,435
a pie, and a bag of oranges

41
00:01:22,435 --> 00:01:24,152
for the stockings.

42
00:01:24,502 --> 00:01:26,988
But prices had gone up that week,

43
00:01:26,988 --> 00:01:27,440
honey.

44
00:01:27,620 --> 00:01:31,176
Nineteen seventy-five was a terrible year for

45
00:01:31,176 --> 00:01:31,730
prices.

46
00:01:31,910 --> 00:01:34,301
Everything was more than I'd figured.

47
00:01:34,481 --> 00:01:36,619
And when the girl at the register

48
00:01:36,619 --> 00:01:38,951
at the Buehler's on Park Avenue rang

49
00:01:38,951 --> 00:01:39,599
it all up,

50
00:01:39,599 --> 00:01:42,255
it came to twenty-eight dollars and forty

51
00:01:42,255 --> 00:01:42,643
cents.

52
00:01:42,823 --> 00:01:45,491
Six dollars and forty cents more than

53
00:01:45,491 --> 00:01:45,923
I had.

54
00:01:46,103 --> 00:01:48,769
I stood there counting my coins again,

55
00:01:48,769 --> 00:01:51,014
like they might have multiplied.

56
00:01:51,194 --> 00:01:53,262
In a minute I'll tell you what

57
00:01:53,262 --> 00:01:54,503
I almost put back.

58
00:01:54,853 --> 00:01:57,762
Behind me, there were six people in

59
00:01:57,762 --> 00:01:59,924
line, all with full carts,

60
00:01:59,924 --> 00:02:01,171
all in a hurry,

61
00:02:01,171 --> 00:02:02,585
on Christmas Eve.

62
00:02:02,765 --> 00:02:05,106
A man in a nice coat sighed

63
00:02:05,106 --> 00:02:05,540
loud.

64
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:06,740
A woman said,

65
00:02:06,740 --> 00:02:09,017
some of us have places to be.

66
00:02:09,197 --> 00:02:12,030
Mark was whining because he was tired.

67
00:02:12,210 --> 00:02:14,900
Susan was tugging my sleeve,

68
00:02:14,900 --> 00:02:16,053
embarrassed.

69
00:02:16,233 --> 00:02:17,843
My face was burning,

70
00:02:17,843 --> 00:02:18,729
sweetheart.

71
00:02:18,909 --> 00:02:20,558
I reached for the ham,

72
00:02:20,558 --> 00:02:23,332
the most expensive thing on the belt.

73
00:02:23,512 --> 00:02:25,586
And then a big hand came down

74
00:02:25,586 --> 00:02:26,658
on the counter.

75
00:02:27,009 --> 00:02:30,220
I remember every face in that line,

76
00:02:30,220 --> 00:02:30,770
honey.

77
00:02:30,950 --> 00:02:32,990
The man in the nice coat had

78
00:02:32,990 --> 00:02:35,029
a cart full of bottles for a

79
00:02:35,029 --> 00:02:35,466
party.

80
00:02:35,646 --> 00:02:37,968
The woman who complained had a fur

81
00:02:37,968 --> 00:02:40,085
collar and a turkey the size of

82
00:02:40,085 --> 00:02:40,836
a suitcase.

83
00:02:41,016 --> 00:02:42,291
And the cashier,

84
00:02:42,291 --> 00:02:44,601
a teenage girl with a red bow

85
00:02:44,601 --> 00:02:45,557
in her hair,

86
00:02:45,557 --> 00:02:48,027
looked at me like she wanted to

87
00:02:48,027 --> 00:02:49,700
help but didn't dare.

88
00:02:49,880 --> 00:02:52,647
Her manager was watching from the office

89
00:02:52,647 --> 00:02:53,131
window.

90
00:02:53,311 --> 00:02:55,839
I'll tell you about that girl later,

91
00:02:55,839 --> 00:02:57,595
because she mattered too.

92
00:02:57,946 --> 00:02:59,381
He was an old man,

93
00:02:59,381 --> 00:03:02,569
maybe seventy, in faded bib overalls and

94
00:03:02,569 --> 00:03:05,360
a barn coat that smelled like cows,

95
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:07,512
with a feed cap pulled low.

96
00:03:07,692 --> 00:03:09,919
He'd been in line behind the lady

97
00:03:09,919 --> 00:03:12,482
who complained, with nothing but a can

98
00:03:12,482 --> 00:03:14,844
of coffee and a bag of peppermints.

99
00:03:15,024 --> 00:03:17,520
He put a ten-dollar bill on the

100
00:03:17,520 --> 00:03:20,258
counter, right on top of my coins,

101
00:03:20,258 --> 00:03:22,432
and he said to the cashier,

102
00:03:22,432 --> 00:03:23,398
ring her up,

103
00:03:23,398 --> 00:03:23,801
miss.

104
00:03:23,981 --> 00:03:25,525
Keep the ham on there.

105
00:03:25,876 --> 00:03:27,355
I said, oh,

106
00:03:27,355 --> 00:03:30,044
no, sir, I couldn't.

107
00:03:30,224 --> 00:03:32,500
And he looked at me from under

108
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:35,307
that cap, with the kindest blue eyes,

109
00:03:35,307 --> 00:03:36,217
and he said,

110
00:03:36,217 --> 00:03:37,886
ma'am, it's Christmas.

111
00:03:38,066 --> 00:03:40,226
Those babies need their ham.

112
00:03:40,406 --> 00:03:42,675
Then he paid for his coffee and

113
00:03:42,675 --> 00:03:45,971
his peppermints, and he handed one peppermint

114
00:03:45,971 --> 00:03:47,801
to Susan and one to Mark.

115
00:03:47,981 --> 00:03:50,175
And he walked out the door into

116
00:03:50,175 --> 00:03:52,511
the snow, before I could even ask

117
00:03:52,511 --> 00:03:53,148
his name.

118
00:03:53,498 --> 00:03:56,247
If you're enjoying Grandma's story,

119
00:03:56,247 --> 00:03:58,369
tap the like button for me,

120
00:03:58,369 --> 00:03:59,233
sweetheart.

121
00:03:59,413 --> 00:04:02,664
It helps more people find these stories.

122
00:04:02,844 --> 00:04:05,262
I ran out into the parking lot

123
00:04:05,262 --> 00:04:07,679
after him, with my bags and my

124
00:04:07,679 --> 00:04:08,405
children.

125
00:04:08,585 --> 00:04:09,816
But he was gone.

126
00:04:09,996 --> 00:04:12,912
Just tire tracks from an old pickup

127
00:04:12,912 --> 00:04:15,661
truck, already filling with snow.

128
00:04:15,841 --> 00:04:18,143
I stood there and cried in that

129
00:04:18,143 --> 00:04:19,034
parking lot.

130
00:04:19,214 --> 00:04:22,023
The lady who'd complained walked past me

131
00:04:22,023 --> 00:04:23,568
and didn't say a word.

132
00:04:23,918 --> 00:04:25,552
When I got home,

133
00:04:25,552 --> 00:04:26,983
I told Harold.

134
00:04:27,163 --> 00:04:28,259
And my Harold,

135
00:04:28,259 --> 00:04:30,843
who was the proudest man in Ohio,

136
00:04:30,843 --> 00:04:33,428
sat down at the kitchen table and

137
00:04:33,428 --> 00:04:35,464
put his face in his hands.

138
00:04:35,644 --> 00:04:36,914
He said, Ruthie,

139
00:04:36,914 --> 00:04:39,693
a stranger had to buy my children's

140
00:04:39,693 --> 00:04:41,042
Christmas dinner.

141
00:04:41,222 --> 00:04:43,046
I said, no,

142
00:04:43,046 --> 00:04:44,206
Harold.

143
00:04:44,386 --> 00:04:46,569
A stranger chose to.

144
00:04:46,749 --> 00:04:47,921
That's different.

145
00:04:48,101 --> 00:04:50,391
And we ate that ham the next

146
00:04:50,391 --> 00:04:52,680
day, and it was the best ham

147
00:04:52,680 --> 00:04:53,825
I ever tasted.

148
00:04:54,005 --> 00:04:57,194
Susan saved the peppermint wrapper in her

149
00:04:57,194 --> 00:04:58,905
jewelry box for years.

150
00:04:59,255 --> 00:05:01,539
I couldn't pay him back,

151
00:05:01,539 --> 00:05:02,111
honey.

152
00:05:02,291 --> 00:05:04,613
I didn't know who he was.

153
00:05:04,793 --> 00:05:06,853
So the next Christmas Eve,

154
00:05:06,853 --> 00:05:10,103
nineteen seventy-six, I went back to that

155
00:05:10,103 --> 00:05:13,432
same Buehler's, with ten dollars I'd saved

156
00:05:13,432 --> 00:05:14,858
in a jar all year.

157
00:05:15,038 --> 00:05:17,253
And I stood near the registers,

158
00:05:17,253 --> 00:05:19,539
pretending to look at magazines,

159
00:05:19,539 --> 00:05:21,682
until I saw a young mother who

160
00:05:21,682 --> 00:05:23,897
looked worried at the checkout.

161
00:05:24,077 --> 00:05:26,092
And when she came up short,

162
00:05:26,092 --> 00:05:28,182
I put the ten dollars on the

163
00:05:28,182 --> 00:05:28,779
counter.

164
00:05:29,129 --> 00:05:31,961
And I said exactly what he'd said.

165
00:05:32,141 --> 00:05:33,511
It's Christmas.

166
00:05:33,691 --> 00:05:35,897
Those babies need their ham.

167
00:05:36,077 --> 00:05:38,683
And I walked out before she could

168
00:05:38,683 --> 00:05:39,630
ask my name.

169
00:05:39,810 --> 00:05:41,789
Then I sat in my car and

170
00:05:41,789 --> 00:05:45,088
cried, because now I understood why he'd

171
00:05:45,088 --> 00:05:46,161
left so fast.

172
00:05:46,341 --> 00:05:47,224
If you stay,

173
00:05:47,224 --> 00:05:48,918
they have to thank you.

174
00:05:49,098 --> 00:05:51,037
And you don't want to be thanked.

175
00:05:51,217 --> 00:05:53,229
You just want them to have their

176
00:05:53,229 --> 00:05:53,481
ham.

177
00:05:53,831 --> 00:05:56,230
I did it every Christmas Eve after

178
00:05:56,230 --> 00:05:57,430
that, sweetheart.

179
00:05:57,610 --> 00:05:59,328
Every single one.

180
00:05:59,508 --> 00:06:01,576
Ten dollars, then twenty,

181
00:06:01,576 --> 00:06:04,472
then fifty, when Harold got his job

182
00:06:04,472 --> 00:06:06,706
back and things got better.

183
00:06:06,886 --> 00:06:08,825
Sometimes groceries.

184
00:06:09,005 --> 00:06:11,680
Once, a whole Christmas dinner for an

185
00:06:11,680 --> 00:06:13,777
old man buying a can of soup.

186
00:06:13,957 --> 00:06:16,279
Harold started coming with me.

187
00:06:16,459 --> 00:06:17,766
Then our children,

188
00:06:17,766 --> 00:06:19,292
when they were grown.

189
00:06:19,472 --> 00:06:21,666
We called it Buehler's Eve.

190
00:06:21,846 --> 00:06:24,435
And we never once told anybody.

191
00:06:24,785 --> 00:06:26,369
Some years were hard,

192
00:06:26,369 --> 00:06:27,199
sweetheart.

193
00:06:27,379 --> 00:06:29,163
In nineteen eighty-two,

194
00:06:29,163 --> 00:06:31,180
Harold was laid off again,

195
00:06:31,180 --> 00:06:33,196
and I had nothing to give.

196
00:06:33,376 --> 00:06:34,695
So I went anyway,

197
00:06:34,695 --> 00:06:37,023
and I helped an old lady carry

198
00:06:37,023 --> 00:06:39,119
her bags to the bus stop in

199
00:06:39,119 --> 00:06:41,447
the snow, and I told her Merry

200
00:06:41,447 --> 00:06:42,223
Christmas.

201
00:06:42,403 --> 00:06:45,003
That counted, I decided.

202
00:06:45,183 --> 00:06:47,273
It doesn't have to be money.

203
00:06:47,453 --> 00:06:49,302
It just has to be the moment

204
00:06:49,302 --> 00:06:52,339
when somebody's standing there with their face

205
00:06:52,339 --> 00:06:53,990
burning, and you step in.

206
00:06:54,340 --> 00:06:57,526
And remember the teenage cashier with the

207
00:06:57,526 --> 00:06:58,148
red bow?

208
00:06:58,328 --> 00:07:00,156
In nineteen eighty-nine,

209
00:07:00,156 --> 00:07:02,136
she was the store manager.

210
00:07:02,316 --> 00:07:04,034
And one Christmas Eve,

211
00:07:04,034 --> 00:07:06,533
she saw me at the registers with

212
00:07:06,533 --> 00:07:08,955
my envelope, and she came over,

213
00:07:08,955 --> 00:07:09,970
and she said,

214
00:07:09,970 --> 00:07:12,625
you're the lady from seventy-five,

215
00:07:12,625 --> 00:07:13,485
aren't you?

216
00:07:13,665 --> 00:07:14,651
I nodded.

217
00:07:14,831 --> 00:07:17,583
She said, I've watched you every year.

218
00:07:17,763 --> 00:07:19,936
And every Christmas Eve since,

219
00:07:19,936 --> 00:07:22,762
she quietly knocked ten percent off the

220
00:07:22,762 --> 00:07:25,298
bill for anybody who came up short.

221
00:07:25,478 --> 00:07:26,634
Stay with me,

222
00:07:26,634 --> 00:07:28,589
honey, it gets better.

223
00:07:28,940 --> 00:07:31,177
So far, the twenty-two dollars,

224
00:07:31,177 --> 00:07:33,198
the farmer, the peppermints,

225
00:07:33,198 --> 00:07:35,580
and forty years of Buehler's Eve.

226
00:07:35,760 --> 00:07:37,582
And every single year,

227
00:07:37,582 --> 00:07:38,990
I looked for him,

228
00:07:38,990 --> 00:07:39,487
honey.

229
00:07:39,667 --> 00:07:41,253
Every Christmas Eve,

230
00:07:41,253 --> 00:07:43,474
I looked at every old man in

231
00:07:43,474 --> 00:07:45,298
overalls in that store.

232
00:07:45,478 --> 00:07:47,417
I asked the managers.

233
00:07:47,597 --> 00:07:50,012
I asked the farmers at church.

234
00:07:50,192 --> 00:07:51,840
Nobody knew him.

235
00:07:52,020 --> 00:07:54,092
And by the nineteen nineties,

236
00:07:54,092 --> 00:07:56,235
I figured he must have passed.

237
00:07:56,415 --> 00:07:59,526
He'd been seventy in nineteen seventy-five.

238
00:07:59,877 --> 00:08:02,408
Harold passed in two thousand eight.

239
00:08:02,588 --> 00:08:04,278
And I kept going,

240
00:08:04,278 --> 00:08:07,359
by myself, every Christmas Eve.

241
00:08:07,539 --> 00:08:09,327
Then with my grandchildren.

242
00:08:09,507 --> 00:08:11,091
And on Christmas Eve,

243
00:08:11,091 --> 00:08:12,676
two thousand fifteen,

244
00:08:12,676 --> 00:08:14,033
I was seventy-one,

245
00:08:14,033 --> 00:08:17,051
standing near the registers at that same

246
00:08:17,051 --> 00:08:19,691
store, which had become a Kroger by

247
00:08:19,691 --> 00:08:22,407
then, with a fifty-dollar bill in my

248
00:08:22,407 --> 00:08:24,821
coat pocket, watching the lines.

249
00:08:25,001 --> 00:08:26,754
Forty years, honey.

250
00:08:26,934 --> 00:08:28,931
The fortieth Buehler's Eve.

251
00:08:29,281 --> 00:08:31,431
There was a young mother at the

252
00:08:31,431 --> 00:08:33,652
register with a baby on her hip,

253
00:08:33,652 --> 00:08:35,039
putting things back.

254
00:08:35,219 --> 00:08:37,328
And I walked up and put my

255
00:08:37,328 --> 00:08:39,031
fifty on the counter,

256
00:08:39,031 --> 00:08:41,059
and said, it's Christmas.

257
00:08:41,239 --> 00:08:43,503
Those babies need their ham.

258
00:08:43,683 --> 00:08:45,610
And I started to walk out.

259
00:08:45,790 --> 00:08:47,105
And the cashier,

260
00:08:47,105 --> 00:08:50,228
a young woman about twenty-five with a

261
00:08:50,228 --> 00:08:51,625
long braid, said,

262
00:08:51,625 --> 00:08:52,036
wait.

263
00:08:52,216 --> 00:08:53,900
Ma'am, wait.

264
00:08:54,080 --> 00:08:56,588
And she came around the register and

265
00:08:56,588 --> 00:08:57,632
grabbed my arm.

266
00:08:57,983 --> 00:09:00,444
She said, what did you just say?

267
00:09:00,624 --> 00:09:02,040
I said it again.

268
00:09:02,220 --> 00:09:04,566
Those babies need their ham.

269
00:09:04,746 --> 00:09:06,805
And that girl went white,

270
00:09:06,805 --> 00:09:07,300
honey.

271
00:09:07,480 --> 00:09:10,855
She said, that's what my great-grandpa used

272
00:09:10,855 --> 00:09:11,404
to say.

273
00:09:11,584 --> 00:09:13,186
Every Christmas Eve.

274
00:09:13,366 --> 00:09:15,676
Grandpa Earl Hoffman.

275
00:09:15,856 --> 00:09:17,878
He had a dairy farm out on

276
00:09:17,878 --> 00:09:19,200
Route Ninety-Six.

277
00:09:19,380 --> 00:09:22,046
He used to pay for people's groceries

278
00:09:22,046 --> 00:09:23,560
at the old Buehler's.

279
00:09:23,740 --> 00:09:26,281
My grandma told me the story a

280
00:09:26,281 --> 00:09:27,467
hundred times.

281
00:09:27,817 --> 00:09:29,076
I sat down,

282
00:09:29,076 --> 00:09:30,336
sweetheart.

283
00:09:30,516 --> 00:09:33,096
Right there on the floor of aisle

284
00:09:33,096 --> 00:09:35,206
three, by the canned beans,

285
00:09:35,206 --> 00:09:36,379
at seventy-one.

286
00:09:36,559 --> 00:09:38,242
Earl Hoffman.

287
00:09:38,422 --> 00:09:41,389
Forty years, and I finally knew his

288
00:09:41,389 --> 00:09:41,813
name.

289
00:09:41,993 --> 00:09:44,043
The cashier sat down next to me,

290
00:09:44,043 --> 00:09:46,285
and the store manager came running,

291
00:09:46,285 --> 00:09:48,464
and the young mother with the baby

292
00:09:48,464 --> 00:09:50,514
stood there with her mouth open.

293
00:09:50,694 --> 00:09:52,079
And the cashier,

294
00:09:52,079 --> 00:09:53,724
her name was Katie,

295
00:09:53,724 --> 00:09:55,629
told me all about him.

296
00:09:55,979 --> 00:09:58,523
Earl Hoffman had lost his wife and

297
00:09:58,523 --> 00:10:01,143
his little daughter in a house fire

298
00:10:01,143 --> 00:10:02,415
on Christmas Eve,

299
00:10:02,415 --> 00:10:04,361
nineteen fifty-two, honey.

300
00:10:04,541 --> 00:10:06,232
They'd been out of money,

301
00:10:06,232 --> 00:10:08,329
and he'd gone into town to sell

302
00:10:08,329 --> 00:10:10,223
a calf, so they could have a

303
00:10:10,223 --> 00:10:11,170
Christmas ham.

304
00:10:11,350 --> 00:10:13,359
He came home to the fire.

305
00:10:13,539 --> 00:10:16,075
And every Christmas Eve for the rest

306
00:10:16,075 --> 00:10:16,920
of his life,

307
00:10:16,920 --> 00:10:19,174
he went to the grocery store and

308
00:10:19,174 --> 00:10:20,865
paid for somebody's ham.

309
00:10:21,045 --> 00:10:22,585
He said it was the ham he

310
00:10:22,585 --> 00:10:24,063
never got to bring home.

311
00:10:24,414 --> 00:10:27,011
He passed in nineteen eighty-four,

312
00:10:27,011 --> 00:10:28,233
at seventy-nine.

313
00:10:28,413 --> 00:10:30,115
Katie said her grandma,

314
00:10:30,115 --> 00:10:31,817
Earl's second daughter,

315
00:10:31,817 --> 00:10:34,406
kept the tradition for a few years,

316
00:10:34,406 --> 00:10:36,034
but then she got sick,

317
00:10:36,034 --> 00:10:37,144
and it stopped.

318
00:10:37,324 --> 00:10:40,861
Katie said, Grandma always wondered if anybody

319
00:10:40,861 --> 00:10:42,014
remembered him.

320
00:10:42,194 --> 00:10:44,725
If it had ever mattered to anybody.

321
00:10:44,905 --> 00:10:46,471
I took Katie's hands,

322
00:10:46,471 --> 00:10:47,292
and I said,

323
00:10:47,292 --> 00:10:49,827
honey, I've done it every year for

324
00:10:49,827 --> 00:10:50,722
forty years.

325
00:10:50,902 --> 00:10:52,156
In his words.

326
00:10:52,336 --> 00:10:53,346
It mattered.

327
00:10:53,696 --> 00:10:56,935
Katie said, my grandma kept his feed

328
00:10:56,935 --> 00:10:57,295
cap.

329
00:10:57,475 --> 00:10:59,684
It's still hanging on a nail in

330
00:10:59,684 --> 00:11:00,540
her kitchen.

331
00:11:00,720 --> 00:11:01,682
And she said,

332
00:11:01,682 --> 00:11:04,719
his truck was a green nineteen fifty-nine

333
00:11:04,719 --> 00:11:07,163
Ford, and he always carried a bag

334
00:11:07,163 --> 00:11:09,533
of peppermints in the glove box,

335
00:11:09,533 --> 00:11:10,495
for children.

336
00:11:10,675 --> 00:11:12,982
I started crying all over again,

337
00:11:12,982 --> 00:11:16,370
because I remembered that peppermint in Susan's

338
00:11:16,370 --> 00:11:17,235
little hand.

339
00:11:17,415 --> 00:11:20,497
Forty years, and the peppermints were still

340
00:11:20,497 --> 00:11:21,142
the same.

341
00:11:21,492 --> 00:11:24,468
Katie took me to see her grandmother,

342
00:11:24,468 --> 00:11:26,237
Earl's daughter, Mary,

343
00:11:26,237 --> 00:11:28,168
the day after Christmas,

344
00:11:28,168 --> 00:11:30,420
at a nursing home in Shelby.

345
00:11:30,600 --> 00:11:31,784
She was ninety.

346
00:11:31,964 --> 00:11:34,479
And when I told her the story,

347
00:11:34,479 --> 00:11:36,576
the ham, the peppermints,

348
00:11:36,576 --> 00:11:39,007
forty years of Buehler's Eve,

349
00:11:39,007 --> 00:11:41,523
she held my hand and cried and

350
00:11:41,523 --> 00:11:44,038
said, Daddy would be so happy.

351
00:11:44,218 --> 00:11:46,134
He always said one ham was a

352
00:11:46,134 --> 00:11:47,434
drop in the bucket.

353
00:11:47,614 --> 00:11:49,618
Now I know it was a whole

354
00:11:49,618 --> 00:11:50,099
river.

355
00:11:50,449 --> 00:11:52,538
Every Christmas Eve now,

356
00:11:52,538 --> 00:11:54,889
Katie and I do it together,

357
00:11:54,889 --> 00:11:55,847
sweetheart.

358
00:11:56,027 --> 00:11:57,235
And her children.

359
00:11:57,415 --> 00:11:58,425
And mine.

360
00:11:58,605 --> 00:12:00,253
And my grandchildren.

361
00:12:00,433 --> 00:12:03,011
Last year, there were fourteen of us,

362
00:12:03,011 --> 00:12:05,867
spread out across three grocery stores in

363
00:12:05,867 --> 00:12:08,305
Mansfield, with envelopes of money.

364
00:12:08,485 --> 00:12:10,986
And every one of us says the

365
00:12:10,986 --> 00:12:11,968
same words.

366
00:12:12,148 --> 00:12:13,588
It's Christmas.

367
00:12:13,768 --> 00:12:15,927
Those babies need their ham.

368
00:12:16,107 --> 00:12:18,344
And every one of us leaves before

369
00:12:18,344 --> 00:12:19,903
they can say thank you.

370
00:12:20,254 --> 00:12:22,979
Here's what I want you to remember,

371
00:12:22,979 --> 00:12:23,446
honey.

372
00:12:23,626 --> 00:12:26,454
You can't always pay back the person

373
00:12:26,454 --> 00:12:27,632
who helped you.

374
00:12:27,812 --> 00:12:30,122
But you can always pay it forward.

375
00:12:30,302 --> 00:12:32,759
And sometimes, if you're lucky,

376
00:12:32,759 --> 00:12:34,900
it finds its way back home.

377
00:12:35,080 --> 00:12:36,854
Tell me in the comments,

378
00:12:36,854 --> 00:12:39,810
sweetheart, has a stranger ever paid for

379
00:12:39,810 --> 00:12:41,140
something for you?

380
00:12:41,320 --> 00:12:43,419
There are more of my family's stories

381
00:12:43,419 --> 00:12:45,404
in the playlist right there on your

382
00:12:45,404 --> 00:12:45,802
screen.

383
00:12:45,982 --> 00:12:46,864
Good night.

384
00:12:47,044 --> 00:12:48,623
Merry Christmas.
